      "text": "To solve the equation \\( f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1}) \\) for the function \\( f(x) = 16x + 3 \\), we start by finding the inverse of \\( f(x) \\).\n\nDefine \\( y = f(x) \\), so \\( y = 16x + 3 \\). To find the inverse, we solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{y - 3}{16}.\n\\]\nThis gives the inverse function:\n\\[\nf^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x - 3}{16}.\n\\]\n\nNext, consider the expression \\( f((2x)^{-1}) \\). First, simplify \\( (2x)^{-1} \\):\n\\[\n(2x)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{2x}.\n\\]\nSubstitute this back into \\( f \\):\n\\[\nf\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) + 3 = \\frac{16}{2x} + 3 = \\frac{8}{x} + 3.\n\\]\n\nNow set the inverse function equal to this expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{x - 3}{16} = \\frac{8}{x} + 3.\n\\]\nTo clear the fractions, multiply both sides by the common denominator, \\( 16x \\):\n\\[\nx(x - 3) = 16 \\left( 8 + 3x \\right).\n\\]\nDistribute and simplify:\n\\[\nx^2 - 3x = 128 + 48x.\n\\]\nMove all terms to one side to form a quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 3x - 48x - 128 = 0 \\implies x^2 - 51x - 128 = 0.\n\\]\nNext, solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula, \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -51 \\), and \\( c = -128 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{(-51)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-128)}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{2601 + 512}}{2} = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{3113}}{2}.\n\\]\nThe sum of the solutions is given by the quadratic equation’s constant term divided by the leading coefficient:\n\\[\n\\frac{-b}{a} = \\frac{-(-51)}{1} = 51.\n\\]\n\nSo, the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{51}.\n\\]",
