      "text": "To find the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f((2x)^{-1}) \\), we need to first express the inverse function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) in terms of \\( x \\).\n\nGiven the function \\( f(x) = 16x + 3 \\), we start by finding its inverse. We solve for \\( x \\) in terms of \\( y \\) where \\( y = f(x) \\):\n\\[ y = 16x + 3 \\]\n\\[ y - 3 = 16x \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{y - 3}{16} \\]\nThus, the inverse function is \\( f^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x - 3}{16} \\).\n\nNext, we substitute \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) into the given equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f((2x)^{-1}) \\):\n\\[ \\frac{x - 3}{16} = f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) \\]\n\nWe need to find \\( f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) \\). Using the definition of \\( f(x) \\), we have:\n\\[ f\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) + 3 = \\frac{16}{2x} + 3 = \\frac{8}{x} + 3 \\]\n\nNow we set up the equation:\n\\[ \\frac{x - 3}{16} = \\frac{8}{x} + 3 \\]\n\nTo clear the fractions, we multiply every term by \\( 16x \\):\n\\[ x(x - 3) = 16 \\cdot 8 + 3 \\cdot 16x \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 3x = 128 + 48x \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 3x - 48x - 128 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 51x - 128 = 0 \\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation. We solve it using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -51 \\), and \\( c = -128 \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{(-51)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-128)}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{2601 + 512}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{3113}}{2} \\]\n\nThe solutions are:\n\\[ x = \\frac{51 + \\sqrt{3113}}{2} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{51 - \\sqrt{3113}}{2} \\]\n\nThe sum of all solutions is:\n\\[ \\frac{51 + \\sqrt{3113}}{2} + \\frac{51 - \\sqrt{3113}}{2} = \\frac{51 + \\sqrt{3113} + 51 - \\sqrt{3113}}{2} = \\frac{102}{2} = 51 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[ \\boxed{51} \\]",
